{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 52, :comments ["<b>For it is a malignant tzara'at</b> — this is the language of \"a stinging briar\" (Ezekiel 28:24). Its midrashic interpretation (Torat Kohanim, Negaim, chapter 14:11) is: give it a curse — that one may not derive benefit from it; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 13:51). And Onkelos (Onkelos on Leviticus 13:52) translated it as סְגִירוּת מְחַזְּרָא, for He made it like a \"stinging briar,\" since in Aramaic they call painful thorns חִזְרֵי, as in חִזְרָא בִּגְבָבָא דְּעַמְרָא (Berakhot 8), שְׁקִילִי טֵיבוּתָךְ וּשְׁדִי אַחִזְרֵי (Shabbat 63). And the truth is that it is the language of a curse, meaning that it is a curse of Elohim upon the garment and upon the house, as I have mentioned (Ramban on Leviticus 13:47). And the midrash that said it is forbidden for benefit — this is derived from the redundancy of the expression. And so too is the law regarding a stricken house, as it is learned from \"and he shall dismantle the house\" (Leviticus 14:45). And thus I found in the Jerusalem Talmud, tractate Orlah (3:3): the stricken stones that were made into lime — there are Tannaim who taught that they have risen from their impurity, and there are Tannaim who taught that they have not risen from their impurity. The one who said they have risen — they are permitted; and the one who said they have not risen — they are forbidden, for it is written \"it is a malignant tzara'at\" (Leviticus 14:44) — put a curse upon it and do not derive benefit from it. Rabbi Abahu said in the name of Rabbi Yohanan: the ashes of all burned things are permitted, except for the ashes of idolatry. Rabbi Hiyya bar Yosei raised an objection before Rabbi Yohanan: but the ashes of the house do not come on account of idolatry, and yet you say they are forbidden? He said to him: it is distinct, for concerning it demolition is written."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "In the Jerusalem Talmud Orlah passage, Ramban's quotation ends mid-exchange; the phrase 'שַׁנְיָה הִיא דִּכְתִיב בֵּהּ נְתִיצָה' means the case of the stricken house is exceptional because Scripture specifies demolition, distinguishing it from the general rule about ashes of burned items."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:42.988245703Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}